[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
Layer-3 connectivity to the data center is healthy; the failure is in name resolution. From host_1_1_1_1 we can ping and HTTP to the data-center IPs (e.g., 10.200.0.3–.6) successfully, but DNS lookups to the site resolver 10.200.0.2 are refused. Evidence: nslookup web_server_0 10.200.0.2 returns “REFUSED” from host_1_1_1_1 (and from other hosts), while /etc/resolv.conf on host_1_1_1_1 points to 10.200.0.2. On dns_server, named is listening on TCP/UDP 53 (netstat shows 10.200.0.2:53 up), but its configuration explicitly disables recursion: /etc/bind/named.conf contains “recursion no;” and there are no authoritative zones for your internal short names (only a default “local” zone). With recursion disabled, BIND refuses queries for names it isn’t authoritative for, so clients perceive that “nothing in the data center answers,” even though the network and servers are reachable by IP.

Root cause: BIND on dns_server was configured as an authoritative-only resolver (recursion no), breaking recursive resolution for client subnets.

Corrective fix: Re-enable recursive DNS for internal clients and/or serve your internal zones. Example in /etc/bind/named.conf or named.conf.options:
options {
  listen-on { any; };
  allow-query { any; };
  recursion yes;
  allow-recursion { 10.1.1.0/24; 10.2.1.0/24; 10.200.0.0/24; };
  forwarders { <upstream-DNS-IPs>; }; // optional
}
Also add/maintain an internal zone with A records for web_server_0–3 if you rely on short names. Then restart named (systemctl restart bind9). 
=== END ===